T08.01|A business combination recognises the acquired business's identifiable assets and liabilities under the applicable acquisition-accounting model. Purchase-price allocation separates identifiable value from residual goodwill. Consideration, non-controlling interests and previously held interests can enter the calculation.~~Buying a collection of assets is not automatically a business combination. Classification affects recognition, costs and goodwill; establish the transaction's nature before applying a formula.|Identifiable assets: Separately recognised qualifying acquired resources.;;Assumed liabilities: Recognised acquired obligations.;;Goodwill: Residual acquisition amount under the governing model.|Assume an acquisition costs ₹120 lakh, with no minority or previously held interest. Identifiable asset fair values are ₹150 and liabilities ₹50.~~Net identifiable assets = ₹100.;;Simplified goodwill = ₹120 − ₹100 = ₹20.;;Allocate depreciation and amortisation using the acquired assets' relevant values and lives.~~The purchase price is not assigned entirely to goodwill.~~Contingent consideration, deferred tax and measurement rules can change real allocations.|Determine whether a business was acquired.;;Value identifiable items and obligations.;;Reconcile consideration and later earnings effects.
T08.02|Goodwill represents the residual recognised in qualifying acquisition accounting. It is not a separately available cash balance. Recoverability testing considers the relevant business units and framework-defined measurement, rather than simply asking whether the acquired company still reports profit.~~Goodwill treatment differs across frameworks and entity regimes. Under the IFRS model, it is not routinely amortised and requires impairment testing; do not assume all jurisdictions use identical rules.|Initial recognition: Acquisition residual.;;Allocation: Assignment to units benefiting from the combination.;;Impairment: Loss when the relevant carrying amount exceeds recoverable value.|Assume a tested unit has carrying amount ₹100 lakh including ₹20 goodwill and recoverable amount ₹85.~~The shortfall is ₹15.;;Under the simplified goodwill-first allocation assumption, goodwill falls to ₹5.;;Recognising this loss does not itself make a current ₹15 cash payment.~~An acquisition can impair even while producing some earnings.~~Actual allocation, testing levels and reversal restrictions follow the applicable standard.|Read acquisition and allocation notes.;;Test forecasts and discount assumptions.;;Separate accounting impairment from acquisition cash paid.
T08.03|Control, joint control and significant influence describe different relationships with another entity. They determine whether consolidation, equity-method or other accounting applies under the relevant framework. Ownership percentage is evidence, not always the sole decision rule.~~Voting arrangements, contractual rights and substantive decision power matter. A minority holding can sometimes convey control, while a substantial percentage may not if rights are restricted.|Subsidiary: Entity controlled by the reporting parent.;;Associate: Significant influence without control.;;Joint arrangement: Contractually shared control with framework-specific classification.|An investor owns 40% of votes, but a substantive agreement requires unanimous consent with another party for relevant decisions.~~Examine the contractual decision rights.;;A 40% percentage alone does not establish control.;;Assess joint control and the arrangement's rights and obligations under the framework.~~Accounting follows the relationship established by substantive facts.~~The example is not a universal percentage threshold or automatic classification.|Read rights and agreements.;;Identify relevant decision-making power.;;Apply the framework's scope and method.
T08.04|Consolidation presents controlled entities as one economic group. Intercompany receivables, payables, revenue, expenses and unrealised internal profits are eliminated so the group does not recognise dealings with itself as outside activity.~~Unrealised inventory profit persists until goods leave the group or another relevant event occurs. Consolidation adjustments need matching records and tax effects where required.|Balance elimination: Remove reciprocal internal claims.;;Transaction elimination: Remove internal revenue and expense.;;Profit elimination: Remove gains not yet realised externally.|A parent sells inventory costing ₹20 lakh to its subsidiary for ₹30. All remains unsold externally at year-end.~~Internal profit is ₹10.;;Eliminate internal sales and purchases.;;Reduce consolidated inventory from the internal ₹30 amount to group cost ₹20, assuming no other measurement change.~~Internal transfer cannot create group profit while the stock remains inside the group.~~External sale and tax consequences require their own subsequent treatment.|Reconcile both entities' records.;;Identify unsold internal assets.;;Check profits, tax and minority effects.
T08.05|Equity-method accounting starts from investment cost and adjusts for the investor's share of relevant investee results and other changes. Dividends typically reduce the investment balance because they distribute previously recognised value rather than creating the same income again.~~Purchase adjustments, accounting-policy alignment, losses, impairment and internal transactions can complicate the method. The investor's share of accounting profit is not the same as cash remitted to it.|Profit share: Investor's portion of relevant investee earnings.;;Dividend receipt: Distribution reducing the investment balance under the model.;;Other adjustments: OCI, acquisition basis and impairment effects.|Investment opens at ₹40 lakh. Assume 25% ownership, investee profit ₹20 and total investee dividends ₹8, with no other changes.~~Recognise profit share ₹5.;;Receive dividends ₹2.;;Closing investment = ₹40 + ₹5 − ₹2 = ₹43.~~Reported investment income is ₹5 while cash received is ₹2.~~Distribution restrictions can leave recognised earnings unavailable for the investor's own payments.|Match ownership and relevant profit.;;Reconcile investment movements.;;Assess distributable cash and impairment separately.
T08.06|Lease accounting reflects a right to use an asset and payment obligations under applicable rules. Under the IFRS lessee model, many leases create a right-of-use asset and lease liability, with defined exemptions. Liability interest and asset depreciation differ from a single cash rental payment.~~Initial measurement includes discounting and framework-specific adjustments. Variable payments, options, modifications and differing frameworks require careful treatment.|Right-of-use asset: Recognised use resource.;;Lease liability: Present value of eligible payments.;;Exempt or variable arrangements: Treatment depends on specified criteria.|Assume two ₹10 lakh year-end payments, 10% discount rate and no initial adjustments.~~Initial liability = 10/1.10 + 10/1.10² = ₹17.36.;;First-year interest ≈ ₹1.74; paying ₹10 reduces liability to about ₹9.09.;;Initial right-of-use asset equals ₹17.36 under the simplified assumptions.~~Cash rent and reported lease expenses follow different schedules.~~The example does not cover every lease or national framework.|Identify lease terms and options.;;Reconcile liability and asset schedules.;;Compare earnings, cash and leverage consistently.
T08.07|Financial instruments are classified using their nature and governing criteria. Under IFRS 9, financial-asset measurement can depend on business model and contractual cash-flow characteristics; other instruments have their own requirements. Classification determines measurement and where changes appear.~~An instrument's label or intent alone is insufficient. Embedded features can change the contractual analysis, and accounting classification need not coincide with its risk-management category.|Amortised cost: Effective-interest measurement for qualifying instruments.;;Fair value through OCI: Specified fair-value category with particular income treatment.;;Fair value through profit or loss: Value changes recognised through earnings under the model.|A bond bought for ₹100 lakh has a ₹95 market value at year-end.~~Establish its contractual features and the relevant business model.;;Determine the measurement category before recording the ₹5 market change.;;Apply that category's interest, impairment and fair-value requirements.~~The market price alone does not determine every accounting entry.~~Current standard requirements and instrument terms govern the classification.|Analyse terms rather than names.;;Read business-model evidence.;;Reconcile measurement, income and impairment.
T08.08|Hedge accounting aligns recognition of a qualifying hedging instrument and hedged item under documented requirements. Economic hedging and hedge-accounting eligibility are distinct: a risk-reducing derivative can still create accounting volatility if it does not qualify.~~Cash-flow, fair-value and net-investment hedges address different exposures. Documentation, eligible items, effectiveness and rebalancing follow the applicable framework rather than a simple assertion that positions offset.|Cash-flow hedge: Exposure to variability in eligible future cash flows.;;Fair-value hedge: Exposure to changes in eligible recognised value.;;Net-investment hedge: Specified foreign-operation investment exposure.|Assume an eligible forecast currency receipt loses ₹5 lakh expected reporting-currency value while a matching derivative gains ₹5.~~Economically, the two effects offset under the simplified case.;;Without qualifying hedge accounting, recognition dates or presentation may differ.;;With qualification, apply the relevant model rather than automatically netting all gains in current profit.~~Economic offset does not by itself settle accounting presentation.~~Ineffectiveness and forecast reliability still need assessment.|Identify the exposure and instrument.;;Check qualification and documentation.;;Separate economic outcome from recognition timing.
T08.09|A foreign-currency transaction creates exposure in an entity's functional currency; translation converts a foreign operation's statements into a presentation currency. Transaction exchange differences and translation effects can have different recognition paths.~~Functional currency reflects the relevant economic environment under the framework, not a freely chosen reporting label. Different rates may apply to balance-sheet items, earnings and equity movements.|Transaction exposure: Foreign-currency monetary claim or obligation.;;Functional currency: Currency of the entity's primary economic environment.;;Translation: Convert statements for presentation.|A firm with rupee functional currency records a $10,000 payable at ₹80/$, then settles at ₹83/$. Assume no hedge.~~Initial payable is ₹800,000.;;Settlement requires ₹830,000.;;The ₹30,000 difference is an exchange loss under the simplified transaction treatment.~~The currency movement changes the payment burden without another purchase.~~Translating an overseas subsidiary involves a different set of accounts and rules.|Identify functional and presentation currencies.;;Separate transactions from foreign-operation translation.;;Reconcile rates, settlement and presentation.
T08.10|Share-based compensation exchanges employee service for equity-linked benefits or cash-settled awards. It can create a recognised expense even without immediate cash payment. Equity dilution, vesting and option value are economic considerations beyond current cash flow.~~Measurement and expense timing depend on award classification and conditions. Adding back the expense in cash-flow analysis does not mean shareholders suffer no cost.|Equity-settled award: Settlement through qualifying equity instruments.;;Cash-settled award: Liability linked to share value.;;Vesting conditions: Requirements affecting entitlement and accounting.|Assume a qualifying award's total service cost is ₹12 lakh recognised evenly over three years.~~Annual expense is ₹4 under the simplified service pattern.;;No immediate cash settlement is assumed for the equity award.;;Assess future dilution or related claim value when valuing common equity.~~Non-cash compensation can still reduce existing owners' economic interest.~~Actual valuation, forfeitures and conditions require the governing award model.|Read award classification and vesting.;;Reconcile expense and outstanding awards.;;Include dilution or option claims consistently in valuation.
T08.11|Defined-contribution plans promise specified contributions; defined-benefit plans promise benefits whose cost depends on assumptions such as salary, longevity and discount rates. A net pension position compares relevant obligation and plan-asset measures under the framework.~~Funding cash and accounting expense differ. Discount-rate or longevity changes can alter obligations without current cash payments, while asset-market movements change funding exposure.|Defined contribution: Employer's qualifying obligation centres on contributions.;;Defined benefit: Employer bears specified benefit-estimation risks.;;Funded versus unfunded: Assets set aside or obligations paid from future resources.|Assume defined-benefit obligation ₹100 lakh and qualifying plan assets ₹80.~~Simplified funding deficit is ₹20.;;If the obligation rises to ₹110 with assets unchanged, the deficit becomes ₹30.;;Assess recognised balance and funding requirements under applicable rules.~~An assumption change can enlarge the burden before additional cash is paid.~~Accounting limits, measurement components and legal funding rules can differ.|Read plan promises and assumptions.;;Separate expense, remeasurement and contributions.;;Stress assets, rates and longevity.
T08.12|Deferred tax tracks specified differences between accounting carrying amounts and tax bases. Tax-loss carryforwards can create potential future tax benefits, but recognition requires the governing conditions and evidence of usable future taxable results.~~An accounting deferred tax asset is not immediately available cash. Forecast utilisation, expiry, restrictions and reversals; optimistic profitability assumptions can overstate recoverability.|Temporary difference: Carrying amount and tax base diverge.;;Deferred tax liability: Qualifying future tax burden.;;Deferred tax asset: Qualifying future deduction or loss benefit.|Assume an asset's accounting carrying amount is ₹80 lakh, tax base ₹60 and applicable illustrative rate 25%.~~Taxable difference = ₹20.;;Simplified deferred tax liability = ₹20 × 25% = ₹5.;;Model reversal and future cash tax instead of treating ₹5 as a payment already made.~~Deferred tax reflects timing or recognition differences.~~Actual exceptions, rates and recognition rules require the applicable framework and jurisdiction.|Reconcile carrying amounts and tax bases.;;Check utilisation evidence and expiry.;;Distinguish accounting tax from cash-tax schedules.
T08.13|Long-term contract accounting depends on the performance obligations and whether criteria for over-time recognition are met. Progress measures should reflect actual transfer of service or control. Cash billing, revenue and profit can occur on different schedules.~~Cost overruns, variable consideration and expected losses need reassessment. A percentage-complete estimate is not permission to recognise revenue merely because management has incurred spending.|Over-time performance: Qualifying transfer recognised as work progresses.;;Point-in-time performance: Recognition at a qualifying transfer date.;;Contract balance: Difference between performance and billing or payment.|Assume a qualifying over-time contract price ₹100 lakh, estimated total cost ₹80 and costs incurred ₹20. Use a justified cost-to-cost measure.~~Progress = 20/80 = 25%.;;Revenue to date = ₹25.;;Profit to date = ₹25 − ₹20 = ₹5 before other effects.~~Incurred cost informs progress only under a suitable measurement method.~~If expected total cost changes, update the measurement and assess loss or other requirements.|Establish recognition criteria first.;;Review progress and cost estimates.;;Reconcile billings, contract assets and obligations.
T08.14|Securitisation transfers or finances pools of claims; derecognition determines whether assets leave the reporting balance sheet under applicable criteria. Legal sale documentation alone may not prove that sufficient risks, rewards or control have transferred for accounting purposes.~~Retained guarantees, servicing and junior interests can leave material exposure. Analyse cash raised, continuing involvement and loss allocation separately.|True-sale analysis: Legal ownership and insolvency treatment.;;Accounting derecognition: Reporting-model transfer assessment.;;Continuing involvement: Retained exposure after a transaction.|A company transfers ₹100 lakh receivables for ₹95 but guarantees the first ₹20 of losses.~~Cash increases ₹95 under the assumed settlement.;;The company still bears specified losses up to ₹20.;;Assess the relevant transfer criteria before removing the full asset from reported accounts.~~Cash funding does not automatically equal full risk transfer.~~The actual guarantee and control terms determine accounting and economic exposure.|Read transfer and guarantee terms.;;Map retained loss exposure.;;Separate legal sale, funding and accounting conclusions.
T08.15|Related-party transactions occur with parties connected through ownership, control, influence or other framework-defined relationships. They can be legitimate, but may be priced or structured differently from independent transactions and deserve transparent analysis.~~A related-party label is not proof of wrongdoing. Examine business purpose, terms, approvals, collectibility and movements rather than inferring fairness from management assurances alone.|Trading transaction: Goods or services with a related party.;;Funding transaction: Loans, guarantees or capital support.;;Asset or ownership transfer: Sale or acquisition between connected parties.|A firm lends ₹20 lakh interest-free to a controlling shareholder's entity while borrowing at 10%.~~Its simplified annual financing burden is ₹2.;;Assess purpose, repayment ability and authorised terms.;;Read disclosure and approval requirements under applicable rules.~~The loan may transfer economic benefit away from the reporting firm.~~A justified commercial arrangement still requires evidence and transparent terms.|Identify relationships and balances.;;Compare terms and business rationale.;;Assess approvals, recoverability and minority-owner effects.
T08.16|Restructuring, disposal and discontinued-operation accounting describe different events. A management plan alone does not automatically create every liability. Asset-disposal gains, restructuring obligations and presentation as discontinued operations each have specific criteria.~~Separate recurring operations from genuinely distinct exits without treating all reorganisation costs as irrelevant. Continuing activities may still bear shared costs after a disposal.|Restructuring provision: Qualifying present obligation under the framework.;;Disposal transaction: Removal or sale of specified assets or business.;;Discontinued operation: Qualifying separate presentation category.|Sell a division for ₹50 lakh whose net carrying amount is ₹35, with ₹5 directly attributable selling costs. Assume no other adjustments.~~Simplified disposal gain = ₹50 − ₹35 − ₹5 = ₹10.;;Cash proceeds before other settlement effects are ₹45.;;Assess presentation and continuing corporate costs separately.~~Disposal profit differs from proceeds and from future operating savings.~~Classification and provisions follow their own rules, not management's description of the sale.|Identify the actual event and obligation.;;Reconcile gain and net proceeds.;;Assess continuing costs and presentation criteria.
